        "page_number": 4,
        "chunk_number": 4,
        "revision": 1,
        "content": "PY-101 / SYNTHETIC COURSE LIBRARY Python Programming Course Handbook Completion requirements The conditions for course completion To complete the Python Programming course, submit all four graded labs, achieve at least 70% overall, and earn a Pass on the capstone project. The overall mark and capstone Pass are evaluated separately. A capstone resubmission is available once after written feedback. The completion record appears in the LMS Results area after the course coordinator verifies the conditions. EduEvidence AI / Synthetic demonstration material Page 4 of 4",
        "score": 0.44078499,
        "rank": 1
      },
      {
        "chunk_id": 11,
        "document_id": 4,
        "document_title": "Full Journey Python Handbook",
        "original_filename": "full-journey-course-handbook.pdf",
        "page_number": 1,
        "chunk_number": 1,
        "revision": 1,
        "content": "PY-101 / SYNTHETIC COURSE LIBRARY Python Programming Course Handbook Course overview Purpose and learning outcomes Python Programming is a six-week foundation course for new programmers. Learners write and debug small command-line programs, organize code into reusable functions, and read structured files. By the end of the course, learners can test functions, handle exceptions, and explain their design decisions in a code review. The course uses the Python standard library and the LMS practice workspace. The course notebook contains examples and extension exercises. EduEvidence AI / Synthetic demonstration material Page 1 of 4",
        "score": 0.17796026,
        "rank": 2
      },
      {
        "chunk_id": 13,
        "document_id": 4,
        "document_title": "Full Journey Python Handbook",
        "original_filename": "full-journey-course-handbook.pdf",
        "page_number": 3,
        "chunk_number": 3,
        "revision": 1,
        "content": "PY-101 / SYNTHETIC COURSE LIBRARY Python Programming Course Handbook Assessment and submission What to upload and where Graded labs account for 60% of the course mark. The capstone project accounts for 40% of the course mark. Submit each lab through the LMS Assessments area as a ZIP archive containing Python source files and a short README. A successful submission generates an LMS receipt. Upload the capstone to the Capstone submission slot. Include your Python source, test files, and instructions for running the project. EduEvidence AI / Synthetic demonstration material Page 3 of 4",
        "score": 0.1445187,
        "rank": 3
      },
      {
        "chunk_id": 12,
        "document_id": 4,
        "document_title": "Full Journey Python Handbook",
        "original_filename": "full-journey-course-handbook.pdf",
        "page_number": 2,
        "chunk_number": 2,
        "revision": 1,
        "content": "PY-101 / SYNTHETIC COURSE LIBRARY Python Programming Course Handbook Modules and weekly practice A six-week learning sequence Weeks 1 and 2 cover variables, control flow, strings, lists, dictionaries, and the interactive Python shell. Weeks 3 and 4 cover functions, modules, files, exceptions, and debugging. Week 5 introduces unit tests and code review. Week 6 is the capstone workshop. Learners bring a working prototype and receive feedback on testing and readability. EduEvidence AI / Synthetic demonstration material Page 2 of 4",
